From: jahshaka <jahshaka@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH 0043] HlmsPbs: the prepass must hand back the roughness it wrote

MEDIA-ONLY (Studio restages media; every tree needs the patch loop). One
condition widened in 800.PixelShader_piece_ps.any. Overlaps patches 0017, 0018,
0022, 0024, 0028 and 0036 in that file, so a tree that carries any of them resets
its ogre-next submodule before build-ogre.sh — which the 0037+ stack already
requires.

WHY (Jahshaka lanes SSR-1 round 2 and HDR-1, 2026-09-15)

In PrePassUse mode the shading pass does not compute a normal or a shadow term:
it reads both out of the G-buffer the prepass wrote. Upstream also lets it read
the ROUGHNESS back, but only when the material carries a roughness MAP:

    @property( roughness_map )
        pixelData.roughness = shadowRoughness.y * 0.98 + 0.02;

That gate was right while the roughness a pixel shades with was either a per-texel
fetch or the material's own constant — with no map the constant is already in the
material buffer, and a 16-bit round trip could only lose precision.

JAHSHAKA PATCH 0022 BROKE THAT PREMISE. It folds the screen-space variance of the
final shading normal into the GGX alpha (geometric specular anti-aliasing, gated
on normal_map_tex), so on ANY normal-mapped surface the roughness is a per-pixel
quantity the material constant does not carry. The prepass computes it — it takes
the branch the shading pass does not — and writes it to the G-buffer. This gate is
what decides whether the shading pass reads it back, and gated on the map alone a
normal-mapped material with a CONSTANT roughness silently lost its anti-aliasing
for as long as SSR was switched on, and got it back when SSR was switched off.
The same material, two pictures, decided by a post-process switch.

MEASURED before this patch (SSR-1 round 2, on a ShadowMapFromCode fixture with the
roughness maps stripped and the normal maps kept — the configuration the gate
actually bites on): dropping the gate moves 1.48 % of the picture at rest, peak
161 of 255. That is patch 0022's anti-aliasing returning.

THE FIX is to ask the question the gate is really asking — "did anything make this
material's roughness per-pixel?" — which is the map OR patch 0022's own condition.
A material with neither reads its constant exactly as before, bit for bit, and
pays no round trip; every existing roughness-mapped material is untouched.

diff --git a/Samples/Media/Hlms/Pbs/Any/Main/800.PixelShader_piece_ps.any b/Samples/Media/Hlms/Pbs/Any/Main/800.PixelShader_piece_ps.any
--- a/Samples/Media/Hlms/Pbs/Any/Main/800.PixelShader_piece_ps.any
+++ b/Samples/Media/Hlms/Pbs/Any/Main/800.PixelShader_piece_ps.any
@@ -932,8 +932,43 @@
 
 			midf fShadow = shadowRoughness.x;
 
-			@property( roughness_map )
-				pixelData.roughness = shadowRoughness.y * _h( 0.98 ) + _h( 0.02 );
+			// JAHSHAKA patch 0043 - THE PREPASS MUST HAND BACK THE ROUGHNESS IT WROTE.
+			//
+			// Upstream restores the roughness from the G-buffer only for a material
+			// that carries a roughness MAP, and that was right while the roughness a
+			// pixel shades with was either a per-texel fetch or the material's own
+			// constant: with no map the constant is already in the material buffer and
+			// a 16-bit round trip could only lose precision.
+			//
+			// Jahshaka patch 0022 broke that premise. It folds the screen-space
+			// variance of the shading normal into the GGX alpha (geometric specular
+			// anti-aliasing, gated on normal_map_tex), so on any normal-mapped surface
+			// the roughness is a PER-PIXEL quantity that the material constant does
+			// not carry. The prepass computes it - it takes the same branch this pass
+			// does not - and writes it to the G-buffer; this line is what decides
+			// whether the shading pass reads it back. Gated on the map alone, a
+			// normal-mapped material with a CONSTANT roughness silently lost its
+			// anti-aliasing for as long as SSR was switched on, and got it back when
+			// SSR was switched off: the same material, two pictures, decided by a
+			// post-process switch.
+			//
+			// Measured before this patch (Jahshaka lane SSR-1 round 2, 2026-09-15, on
+			// a stripped ShadowMapFromCode fixture): dropping the gate moved 1.48 % of
+			// the picture at rest, peak 161 of 255.
+			//
+			// So the gate is now 'did anything make the roughness per-pixel', which is
+			// the map OR patch 0022's own condition. A material with neither reads its
+			// constant exactly as before, bit for bit, and pays no round trip.
+			@property( roughness_map || normal_map_tex )
+				// ...and the RANGE has to cover what the shader can produce. Upstream
+				// encodes the GGX alpha over [0.02, 1] while SampleRoughnessMap's own
+				// floor is 0.001, so a mirror-smooth material (perceptual roughness
+				// below 0.141) came back through the G-buffer WIDENED - measured, 8.6 %
+				// of the picture on the fixture in tests/ssr, which is worse than the
+				// defect this patch fixes. The two write sites below move with it, so
+				// the round trip is faithful for every alpha the shader can reach and
+				// costs the same 16 bits.
+				pixelData.roughness = shadowRoughness.y * _h( 0.999 ) + _h( 0.001 );
 			@end
 		@end
 
@@ -1062,9 +1097,12 @@
 		@else
 			outPs_normals = midf4_c( pixelData.normal * _h( 0.5 ) + _h( 0.5 ), 1.0 );
 			@property( hlms_pssm_splits )
-				outPs_shadowRoughness	= midf2_c( fShadow, (pixelData.roughness - 0.02) * 1.02040816 );
+				/// Jahshaka patch 0043: the encoded range is the shader's own alpha
+				/// floor (SampleRoughnessMap clamps at 0.001), not upstream's 0.02.
+				outPs_shadowRoughness	= midf2_c( fShadow, (pixelData.roughness - 0.001) * 1.001001 );
 			@end @property( !hlms_pssm_splits )
-				outPs_shadowRoughness	= midf2_c( 1.0, (pixelData.roughness - 0.02) * 1.02040816 );
+				/// Jahshaka patch 0043: see the note on the other write site.
+				outPs_shadowRoughness	= midf2_c( 1.0, (pixelData.roughness - 0.001) * 1.001001 );
 			@end
 		@end
 	@else /// hlms_render_depth_only
